<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Girnar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mount Girnar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Girnar is a group of volcanic peaks near Junagadh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, sacred to Jains and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>. A rock at its foot carries inscriptions of the Mauryan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satrap</a> Rudradaman and of the Gupta period. Its slopes hold Jain temples, including those dedicated to Neminatha and the shrines built by the ministers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastupala">Vastupala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tejapala">Tejapala</a> in the 13th century, and thousands of pilgrims climb its steps.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/girnar.jpg" alt="Girnar" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sachinvenga · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dattatreya_Temple_of_Girnar.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pilgrimage to sacred places such as Shatrunjaya, <b>Mount Girnar</b> and Sammed Shikharji, where twenty of the Tirthankaras are said to have attained liberation, is a lifelong aspiration.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nawabs governed a mostly Hindu population that included the sacred <b>Girnar</b> hill and the temple of Somnath.</blockquote><a href="/state/junagadh-state">Junagadh State</a></li><li><blockquote>The ministers Vastupala and Tejapala, serving Viradhavala, are famous for building Jain temples at Mount Abu, notably the Luna Vasahi at Dilwara, and at <b>Girnar</b> and for their patronage of literature.</blockquote><a href="/state/vaghela-dynasty">Vaghela dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
